        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Celts</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland, with offshoots on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> (Galatia)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a> c. 800–450 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> from c. 450 BCE to the Roman conquest (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> 58–50 BCE, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> 43–84 CE); Ireland was never conquered, and Celtic languages live on</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The salt mines and princely burials of the Hallstatt world; La Tène art; hillforts and the towns called oppida; the druids; the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> c. 390 BCE and the resistance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Greek and Roman writers (Hecataeus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a> through Diodorus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Caesar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>); inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepontic-language">Lepontic</a>, Celtiberian and Gaulish; coins; excavated sites such as Hallstatt, La Tène, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vix-grave">Vix</a>, Manching and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a>; medieval Irish and Welsh literature</span></div>
        </div>
