    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>“Celts” was a Greek name for peoples of the European interior; today it is used above all for speakers of Celtic languages, who in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and Ireland to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</li><li>Archaeologists associate them with the Hallstatt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène cultures</a>, known for salt mines, rich princely burials, hillforts, the walled towns called oppida and a flowing, abstract art.</li><li>Celtic war bands sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and attacked Delphi, but Rome conquered most Celtic lands from the 3rd century BCE to the 1st century CE; the languages survive in Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Brittany.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Greek bronze krater, a vessel for mixing wine, found in a princely grave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vix-grave">Vix</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> stands 1.64 metres high, weighs 208.6 kilograms and holds about 1,100 litres: the largest Greek bronze vessel known.</p></aside>
    </section>

