    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 800 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">Iron working</a> spreads in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a> begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 600 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> found Massalia; Greek wine and bronzes reach the Hallstatt princes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 450 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> and its art style emerge between Champagne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 390 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-3rd-century-bce">Brennus</a> rout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the Allia and sack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">279–278 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Celtic army attacks Delphi; three tribes cross into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and settle in Galatia</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">225 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> destroys a Gallic army at Telamon and goes on to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">58–50 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> conquers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> surrenders at Alesia in 52 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">43–84 CE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquers most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, crushing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>’s revolt and winning at Mons Graupius</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>“Celts” comes from Keltoi, the name Greek writers gave to peoples of the European interior. About 500 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a> placed Massalia, modern Marseille, near the land of the Celts, and in the 5th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> wrote that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> rises among the Celts and that Celts lived in the far west, beyond the Pillars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> also called them Galatai, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> Galli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> wrote that the people of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> called themselves Celtae in their own language. No ancient writer called the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland Celts. That usage began when the Welsh scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-lhuyd">Edward Lhuyd</a> showed in his Archaeologia Britannica (1707) that Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh, Cornish and Breton were related, linked them with what was known of the speech of the ancient Gauls and Celtiberians, and called the family Celtic.</p><p>The word is thus used in several senses, and how far they belong together is debated. Linguists use it for a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>, archaeologists for the Hallstatt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène cultures</a> and their art. Since the 1990s scholars such as Simon James and John Collis have argued that the idea of one Celtic people, with a single homeland and culture spread by migration, is largely a modern construction, and that the term is best kept for the languages; others reply that shared speech, art, gods and institutions make it a useful name for many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> peoples. Where Celtic speech arose is also disputed. The traditional view places it in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, spreading with the Hallstatt and La Tène cultures; Barry Cunliffe and John Koch have argued instead for the Atlantic coasts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. The oldest inscriptions generally accepted as Celtic, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepontic-language">Lepontic</a>, come from around the Italian lakes and date from about the 6th century BCE.</p><p>Archaeologically, the story begins with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a>, which grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> Urnfield culture of central Europe as iron working spread from about 800 BCE. It is named after a cemetery above the salt mines of Hallstatt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, where Johann Georg Ramsauer began digging in 1846 and more than a thousand graves have since been found; the salt in the mines preserved textiles, wooden tools, shoes and leather backpacks. Archaeologists divide it into Hallstatt C, about 800–620 BCE, and Hallstatt D, about 620–450 BCE. Around 450 BCE the La Tène culture took its place, named after a site on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-neuchatel">Lake Neuchâtel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> where many weapons and other objects had been placed in the water.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vix-grave"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vix-grave.jpg" alt="Vix Grave" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vix-grave">Vix Grave</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chatillon-sur-Seine_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Pays_chatillonnais_-_Crat%C3%A8re_de_Vix_-_01_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christophe Finot, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 6th and early 5th centuries BCE power in a zone from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> lay with rulers based at fortified hilltop seats, such as the Heuneburg on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, which in the 6th century BCE was given a wall of mud brick on stone footings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> manner, and Mont Lassois in Burgundy. Their burials were lavish. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, a man buried about 530 BCE lay on a bronze couch almost three metres long, carried by eight small bronze women on wheels, with a four-wheeled wagon, drinking horns for nine, gold plaques on his shoes and a bronze cauldron made in the Greek cities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> that had held some 400 litres of mead. Below Mont Lassois, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vix-grave">Vix</a>, a person of rank, usually identified as a woman, was buried in the late 6th or early 5th century BCE with a gold torc and a giant Greek bronze krater.</p><p>After about 450 BCE the princely seats were abandoned and power passed to warrior elites farther north and west, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, who were buried with two-wheeled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>. Greek and Roman writers describe societies of nobles, their clients and their war bands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a>, a Greek who travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> about 100 BCE and whose account survives through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Athenaeus, wrote of feasts at which the bravest man claimed the best cut of meat and rivals fought him for it, and Diodorus says that Italian merchants could get a slave for a jar of wine. Most people were farmers who grew wheat and barley and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, pigs and sheep in farmsteads and villages; in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> many looked to hillforts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danebury">Danebury</a> and Maiden Castle.</p><p>From the 2nd century BCE large fortified towns, which Caesar called oppida, grew up from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, among them Manching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a>, the hill town of the Aedui on Mont Beuvray. Behind ramparts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, stone and earth lived craftsmen, traders and nobles, and many peoples struck coins. In Caesar’s day the larger Gallic peoples, such as the Aedui, were governed by councils of nobles and elected magistrates; Caesar names the druids and the knights as the two honoured classes, with the common people little better than slaves. Women of high rank could hold power: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> ruled the Brigantes of northern Britain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> led the Iceni in revolt.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron.jpg" alt="Gundestrup cauldron" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron">Gundestrup cauldron</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gundestrupkarret_F.I.7074b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nationalmuseet, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Celtic religion is known from three kinds of evidence, none of them complete: Greek and Roman writers, who described it as outsiders; archaeology; and inscriptions and medieval Irish and Welsh literature, written down by Christians long after. Caesar presents the druids as the priests and learned class of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. They conducted sacrifices and judged disputes, were exempt from war and taxes and taught that souls do not die but pass after death from one body to another. Their training could last twenty years, since they learned their lore by heart rather than writing it down, and each year they met in the land of the Carnutes, thought to be the centre of Gaul; Caesar believed the institution had begun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> describes a druid in a white robe cutting mistletoe from an oak with a golden sickle before two white bulls were sacrificed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> suppressed the druids under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> describes the Roman attack in 60 or 61 CE on their stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, where black-robed women with torches stood among the defenders.</p><p>Caesar matched the gods of Gaul with Roman ones, above all Mercury, whom he calls the inventor of all arts, and the poet Lucan names three Gaulish gods, Teutates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esus">Esus</a> and Taranis. Inscriptions record hundreds of divine names, many of them local: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a>, remembered in the name of Lugdunum, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>; Epona, the horse goddess, popular in the Roman army; Cernunnos, named on a carved pillar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>; and Sulis, goddess of the hot springs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>. An antlered figure on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundestrup-cauldron">Gundestrup cauldron</a>, found in a Danish bog in 1891 and made, most scholars think, by Thracian silversmiths in the 2nd or 1st century BCE, is commonly identified with Cernunnos.</p><p>Archaeology shows offerings in water and in sanctuaries. Weapons, cauldrons and ornaments were thrown into lakes, rivers and bogs, from La Tène itself to the Thames, and at enclosures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gournay-sur-aronde">Gournay-sur-Aronde</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> thousands of deliberately broken weapons were displayed with the bones of sacrificed animals and of people. Greek writers describe warriors keeping the heads of enemies, and at Entremont in Provence real skulls were set in a portico. Roman authors accuse the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>; some finds, such as Lindow Man, killed and laid in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a> bog probably in the 1st century CE and found in 1984, may support them, but how common the practice was is debated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battersea-shield"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battersea-shield.jpg" alt="Battersea Shield" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battersea-shield">Battersea Shield</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Battersea_Shield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BabelStone, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hallstatt craftsmen favoured geometric patterns, and their rulers prized imported Greek and Etruscan wares. From about 450 BCE La Tène artists created a new style. They took the palmettes, lotus flowers and tendrils of Greek and Etruscan ornament and turned them into flowing, asymmetrical curves in which faces and animals appear and dissolve. In Early Celtic Art (1944) the scholar Paul Jacobsthal divided its development into an Early Style, a Waldalgesheim Style named after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> burial in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, and later Plastic and Sword Styles, a scheme still widely used. The finest works are in metal: the bronze flagons from Basse-Yutz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, inlaid with coral and enamel; the gold-covered helmet from Agris; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battersea-shield">Battersea Shield</a>, the Witham Shield and the horned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterloo-helmet">Waterloo Helmet</a> from British rivers; bronze mirrors engraved with spirals; and gold torcs, the neck rings worn by nobles and shown on gods, such as the Great Torc from Snettisham.</p><p>Sculpture in stone was rarer but striking, like the life-size sandstone warrior of the 5th century BCE found in 1996 beside a burial mound at the Glauberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, wearing a crown shaped like two great leaves. Smiths forged long iron swords and the iron tyres of chariots, and from the 3rd century BCE many peoples struck coins, first copying the gold staters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and then turning the king’s head and chariot into ever more abstract designs.</p><p>The Celtic peoples wrote little. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepontic-language">Lepontic</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> used an alphabet adapted from the Etruscan, Celtiberian was written in the Iberian script, and in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> Gaulish was written in Greek letters; Caesar found lists of the Helvetii written in Greek letters. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> Gaulish was written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, as on the bronze calendar found at Coligny in 1897, probably engraved in the late 2nd century CE, which sets out a five-year cycle of lunar months marked as good or not good. Caesar says that the druids refused to commit their teaching to writing, and law, genealogy, history and poetry were kept in the memory of learned men; in Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> this oral learning lasted into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and from about the 4th century CE the Irish cut short inscriptions in ogham, an alphabet of strokes along the edge of a stone.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dying-gaul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dying-gaul.jpg" alt="Dying Gaul" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dying-gaul">Dying Gaul</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Galata_Morente_-_Musei_Capitolini_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Pol GRANDMONT This illustration was made by Jean-Pol GRANDMONT Please credit this : Jean-Pol GRANDMONT An e..., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Celtic world was tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> from early on. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> founded Massalia about 600 BCE, and Greek wine, bronze vessels and painted pottery travelled up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> to the Hallstatt princes, while Etruscan beaked flagons crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to the first La Tène nobles; about 325 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pytheas">Pytheas of Massalia</a> sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and beyond. In the 5th and 4th centuries BCE Celtic peoples crossed the Alps into the Po valley, where the Insubres, Boii and Senones settled, and in about 390 BCE a Gallic army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-4th-century-bce">Brennus</a> routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the Allia and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> tells that when the Romans protested at the weights used for their ransom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-3rd-century-bce">Brennus</a> threw his sword onto the scales with the words “Woe to the vanquished”.</p><p>Celtic bands served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a>, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and for Hellenistic kings. Others moved down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In 279 BCE a second Brennus led an army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and attacked Delphi, and in 278 BCE three tribes crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> at the invitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia">Nicomedes I of Bithynia</a> and settled in the region later named Galatia after them; the victories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> over the Galatians were celebrated with statues such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dying-gaul">Dying Gaul</a>, and in the 4th century CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Saint Jerome</a> wrote that the Galatians still spoke a language close to that of the Treveri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. In central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the Celtiberians combined Celtic speech with Iberian ways.</p><p>From the late 3rd century BCE Rome turned the tide. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> destroyed a Gallic army at Telamon in 225 BCE, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> by the early 2nd century BCE, fought the Celtiberians until the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> in 133 BCE and made southern Gaul the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> about 121 BCE. Between 58 and 50 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> conquered the rest of Gaul; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> of the Arverni united most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> against him in 52 BCE and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>, but surrendered at Alesia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> wrote that in these wars Caesar killed a million men and took a million captive, figures that cannot be checked but show the scale of the slaughter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> invaded Britain in 43 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>’s revolt of 60 or 61 CE was crushed, and in 83 or 84 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> defeated the Caledonians at Mons Graupius, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> has their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a> say of the Romans, “they make a desert and call it peace”. Ireland was never conquered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vercingetorix.jpg" alt="Vercingetorix" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercing%C3%A9torix_stat%C3%A8re_Gallica_avers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the province of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> filled with towns, villas and roads. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallo-romans">Gallo-Romans</a> honoured Celtic gods under Roman names in Roman-style temples, and Gaulish was still spoken in places in late antiquity, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, the ancestor of French, prevailed. Celtic names survive on the map: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> from Lugdunum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from the Parisii, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from the Boii, and many rivers and hills.</p><p>Beyond the frontier, in Ireland and northern Britain, Celtic-speaking societies continued without Roman rule. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emain-macha">Navan Fort</a> in Ulster, the Emain Macha of legend, a great round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> building was raised about 95 BCE, then filled with stones and burned. When Irish monks wrote down the tales of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulster-cycle">Ulster Cycle</a>, among them the Táin Bó Cúailnge, they preserved a world of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warriors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raids and feasts that the scholar Kenneth Jackson called “a window on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>” (1964), though most scholars now read the tales chiefly as literature of their own Christian centuries. Between the 4th and 7th centuries Britons crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a>, which became Brittany, and Gaelic spread from Ireland to much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and to the Isle of Man. The Celtic languages still spoken are Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh and Breton; Manx, whose last native speaker died in 1974, and Cornish have been revived.</p><p>The modern idea of the Celts was born with Lhuyd and grew with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. James Macpherson’s poems of Ossian, published in the 1760s as translations of ancient Gaelic verse but largely his own, were read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; Iolo Morganwg devised the bardic Gorsedd in 1792; and national movements in Ireland, Scotland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Brittany looked to the Celts as ancestors. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> had Alesia excavated and in 1865 raised a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> by Aimé Millet on the site; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> a bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> has stood beside Westminster Bridge since 1902. Today scholars use the word with more care, excavation and ancient DNA are refining the story of who moved where and when, and La Tène art remains the source of much that is called “Celtic” design.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Celts were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> peoples of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> who spoke Celtic languages and are associated with the Hallstatt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène cultures</a>. Their princes traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, their warriors sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and settled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and their druids kept learning in memory. Rome conquered most of their lands, but their art, place names and languages endure.</em></p>

